--expect
7 48879 66 7 23713 7 | 7 7 49374 170 | 1 2 2 0 | 10 roots=0 max=6
--expect-why
DERIVED from `mapread.kin` against the driver's stubs. The fixture's hex
constants in decimal: 0xBEEF = 48879, 0x42 = 66, 0x5CA1 = 23713, 0xC0DE =
49374, NOT_FOUND = 0xFFFE = 65534. The stubs are `hash-value v = (v*7) & 0xff`,
`node-find-scalar` answering 0x5CA1 only for hash 0x77, `node-find` answering
0xC0DE only for hash 0x77, `ref-get` always 0xBEEF, and `eq-may-alloc v` =
`v >= 900`, which is what routes a lookup to the scalar path or the rooted one.

THE SEVEN `map-get` ANSWERS, each taking a different arm:

  7        an IMMEDIATE (5). `is-heap` is the first test in `map-get`, so the
           default comes straight back without reading a type byte.
  48879    a ROW REF. The tag test comes BEFORE both representation arms, so
           the ref is handed to `ref-get` and answers 0xBEEF. The source's
           comment says why it is here and not at every call site: `is-map` is
           true for a ref, so any path that asked and then reached a map
           internal would read a ref as an array map and find nothing -- which
           is what `(:name row)` once did. A missing arm prints `7` here, and
           7 is a plausible-looking default.
  66       an array map, scalar key 0x41, FOUND at index 1: `am-val 1` is
           h[105] = 0x42. Not 0x32 (index 0's value), which is what an
           `am-val` reading the wrong pair would give.
  7        the same map, key 0x99, absent: `am-index-of` answers `n` = 2, which
           `map-get` compares against `map-count` and turns into the default.
           THE SENTINEL IS THE POINT: `n` is outside `0..n-1`, so no signed
           type and no `Option` is needed -- and a source that answered 0 or
           -1 for "absent" would print `0x32` or index out of range here.
  23713    a hash map, scalar key 0x11. 0x11 * 7 = 0x77, so the stub finds it.
           This is the SCALAR walk: 23713 is `node-find-scalar`'s answer and
           49374 would be `node-find`'s, so this number says which of the two
           the source called. Routing a scalar key through the rooted walk is
           correct-but-slow, and the header measures it at about 6% of lookup
           time; this field is what would catch it.
  7        a hash map whose HM_ROOT slot is NIL -- an empty map. The root is
           checked BEFORE hashing, and the default comes back rather than the
           walk being handed nil.

  | 7      a COMPOUND key (900) in the ARRAY MAP. It takes the rooted arm of
           both `map-get` and `am-index-of`, scans both pairs, matches
           neither, and answers the default. The bar separates the compound
           calls.
  7        a compound key in the HASH map. 900 * 7 = 6300, and 6300 & 0xff =
           156, which is NOT 0x77 -- so the stub answers NOT_FOUND and the
           default comes back. This is the compound MISS; the two fields after
           it are the compound HITS, and until 2026-09-26 neither existed.
  49374    a compound key that IS found in a HASH map. 1041 * 7 = 7287 and
           7287 & 0xff = 0x77, so the stub answers 0xC0DE -- which is
           `node-find`'s success value and NOT `node-find-scalar`'s 0x5CA1, so
           this field says the rooted walk was the one called. `(set out got)`
           in the compound hash-map arm was unreachable from this fixture
           before: every compound lookup missed, so a source that dropped the
           found value and always answered the default passed.
  170      a compound key found in an ARRAY map -- 0xAA, the value at index 0
           of the map at 320. Two arms were unreachable behind this one call:
           `map-get`'s `(set out (am-val ...))` in the compound array arm, and
           `am-index-of`'s `(set out i)` plus `(break)` in the rooted scan.
           THE 320 MAP HAS THE SAME KEY TWICE, (1041 -> 0xAA) then
           (1041 -> 0xBB), which `assoc` makes impossible in a real map. It is
           the only way `break` is OBSERVABLE: with it `out` stays 0 and the
           answer is 0xAA, without it the scan runs on, `out` becomes 1 and the
           answer is 0xBB. A single matching entry cannot tell the two apart.

  | 1 2 2  `am-index-of` called directly.
           1  key 0x41 is at index 1.
           2  key 0x99 is absent, so `n` = 2 -- the sentinel again, printed
              here as the function's own answer rather than filtered through
              `map-get`.
           2  the same absence through the COMPOUND path. Both paths must
              answer the same sentinel; the scalar one returns `sn` and the
              rooted one returns `out` initialised to `n`, which are two
              separate lines of source that have to agree.
           0  PRESENCE through the compound path, on the 320 map. The three
              fields before it are all absences or scalar hits, so this is the
              only one that reads the rooted scan's `out` after it was assigned
              rather than after it was initialised.

  | 10     membership: 0x31 is in the map, 0x99 is not. `map-contains` is
           `map-get` against NOT_FOUND, so the `0` also says NOT_FOUND is
           never a legitimate answer leaking out of a lookup.

  roots=0  after all sixteen calls the root stack is empty. Every rooted path
           -- `map-get`'s compound arm, `am-index-of`'s compound arm, and the
           per-comparison push inside it -- pops what it pushed, on the found
           path and the absent path alike.

  max=6    the DEEPEST the stack ever got, and it is derivable exactly. The
           three calls added in 2026-09-26 do not raise it: a compound hash-map
           lookup pushes only map, key and default (3), and the compound
           array-map lookup on the 320 map reaches the same 6 by the same
           route. The worst case is the compound array-map lookup: `map-get` pushes map,
           key and default (3), then `am-index-of` pushes map and key again
           (5), then pushes the key being compared (6) and pops it before the
           next comparison. 6 and not 7 says the per-comparison push does not
           accumulate across the scan -- an n-entry map must not need n roots.
           6 and not 5 says the compared key is rooted at all, which is what
           makes `val-eq` safe when it can allocate.
